 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE RING OF BRODGAR. A great circle of standing stones, 104 metres wide, raised between 2500 and 2000 BC. There were once 60; twenty-seven still stand. Nobody knows why they built it, but they surveyed it with great care."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE WATCH STONE. A lone, tall slab nearby. People have made guesses about it for centuries. Mine is that it's watching the ring, and the ring is watching the sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "KIRKWALL (Kirkjuvagr). 'Church Bay' in Old Norse. Orkney was Norse until 1468, when it was pledged to the Scottish king as part of a wedding dowry. Nobody has ever asked for it back."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "ST MAGNUS CATHEDRAL. Begun in 1137 by Earl Rognvald in memory of his uncle Magnus, in red and yellow sandstone. It took 300 years. Both men's bones were later found tucked away inside its pillars."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE BA' GAME. On Christmas Day and New Year's Day the Uppies and the Doonies push a leather ball through the streets for hours. The goals are the harbour and a wall. Shops board up their windows. All very polite."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE ROAD TO BRODGAR. Stones, birds, light, and not many trees. Skara Brae, a village of stone houses older than the pyramids, is a short drive away. You are standing in one of the great Neolithic landscapes of the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "FERRY TO ABERDEEN. The Hjaltland Gull takes six hours, and the sea is rarely calm. The return trip costs just as many sandwiches."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: St Magnus Cathedral, just up the street, was founded in 1137 by Earl Rognvald in memory of his uncle Magnus.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "kirkwall_st_magnus",
   "title": "St Magnus Cathedral"
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "kirkwall_name_orkney",
   "title": "Name: Orkney",
   "text": "The Romans and Greeks called these islands the Orcades, probably from a Celtic word for a young pig or boar, perhaps a tribe's emblem. The Norse heard their own word orkn, a seal, and added ey, islands. So: Seal Islands. The seals have never accepted the pig theory."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "kirkwall_maeshowe",
   "people": "norse",
   "title": "Maeshowe Runes",
   "text": "Maeshowe, a great chambered tomb nearly 5,000 years old, is lined up so the midwinter sunset shines down its passage. In the 1100s Norsemen broke in and covered the walls in runes: names, boasts and claims of hidden treasure. It is some of the finest Viking graffiti in the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "kirkwall_italian_chapel",
   "title": "The Italian Chapel",
   "text": "In 1939 a U-boat slipped into Scapa Flow and sank HMS Royal Oak, and over 800 men died. Barriers were built to seal the channels, partly by Italian prisoners of war, who also turned two Nissen huts on Lamb Holm into a beautifully painted chapel. It still stands, and still takes your breath away."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "kirkwall_john_rae",
   "title": "John Rae",
   "text": "John Rae, born in Orkney in 1813, became one of the great Arctic explorers by travelling as the Inuit did, on foot and on snowshoes. In 1854 he brought back the first news of the lost Franklin expedition. Victorian London did not like what he reported, and it was a very long time before he got proper credit."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "arts_mackay_brown",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "George Mackay Brown",
   "text": "George Mackay Brown was born in Stromness, across the island, in 1921, and hardly ever left it. Poems, stories and novels, Greenvoe in 1972 among them, turned Orkney's fishermen, crofters and Norse ghosts into something read across the world; Beside the Ocean of Time was shortlisted for the Booker Prize in 1994. He wrote in the mornings, walked to the pier in the afternoons and died in 1996. The pier is still there, and so are the gulls who thought he was feeding them."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "arts_st_magnus_festival",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The St Magnus Festival",
   "text": "The composer Peter Maxwell Davies came to Orkney in 1971, settled on Hoy and later on Sanday, and in 1977 founded the St Magnus Festival with George Mackay Brown: a week of music each midsummer in the cathedral and the island halls. His piano piece 'Farewell to Stromness', from 1980, was a protest against a planned uranium mine. He was Master of the Queen's Music from 2004 to 2014 and died in 2016. The festival still runs, and the light at midsummer barely goes out."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "landsky_old_man_of_hoy",
   "title": "The Old Man of Hoy",
   "text": "Off the west coast of Hoy stands a sea stack 137 metres tall, Old Red Sandstone laid down in a vast lake nearly 400 million years ago, on a plinth of harder basalt. He is young, as rocks go: maps from the 1750s show a headland, and a drawing of 1817 shows an arch with two legs; the sea took one of them in a storm. He was first climbed in 1966 by Chris Bonington, Tom Patey and Rusty Baillie, and the BBC broadcast a climb live the next summer to millions of viewers."
  }
